# D1 follow-on, found by this pass: with the drafted amd.conf.xml the
# LONGGREETING trigger can almost never fire on real speech.
#
# PR1's fix for D1 raised greeting 2500 -> 5000 (so a person who answers
# with a sentence in one breath, 2.6-3 s of voice, is not called a
# MACHINE) and left total_analysis_time at 7000.
#
# In mod_amd, voice_duration accumulates only on frames at or above
# silence_threshold, and is reset by any silence of between_words_silence
# (250 ms) or more. So LONGGREETING needs 5000 ms of ACCUMULATED VOICE
# inside a 7000 ms wall-clock window - a voice density above about 71%.
# Ordinary speech does not reach that: the gaps between words are 120-180
# ms, below the 250 ms reset but still silence, so they do not count.
#
# Measured on the lab's own audio (amd-sim.py, the port of webitel/mod_amd
# 52e6bc6 used to pick these values), through the same G.711 round trip:
#
#   clip                       inter-word gaps      voice_duration hits 5000
#   13.4 s continuous greeting 120-180 ms, 2 >=250  at t = 7.22 s
#
#   total_analysis_time =  7 s  -> NOTSURE/TOOLONG at 7.00 s   <-- drafted
#   total_analysis_time =  9 s  -> MACHINE/LONGGREETING at 7.22 s
#   total_analysis_time = 12 s  -> MACHINE/LONGGREETING at 7.22 s
#
# That clip is the easiest machine there is: one unbroken 13 s sentence,
# no pauses between sentences at all. It still misses by 220 ms.
#
# Every mailbox clip in the lab therefore comes out NOTSURE/TOOLONG at
# 7.02 s under the drafted config:
#
#   human_hello        HUMAN   SILENCEAFTERGREETING 1.60
#   human_long         HUMAN   SILENCEAFTERGREETING 4.22
#   human_phrases      HUMAN   SILENCEAFTERGREETING 3.54   (the D1 case)
#   silent_then_hello  NOTSURE INITIALSILENCE       2.50
#   vm_beep_short      NOTSURE TOOLONG              7.02
#   vm_beep_long       NOTSURE TOOLONG              7.02
#   vm_beep_1200ms     NOTSURE TOOLONG              7.02
#   vm_beep_noisy      NOTSURE TOOLONG              7.02
#   vm_nobeep          NOTSURE TOOLONG              7.02
#   beep_only          HUMAN   SILENCEAFTERGREETING 2.30
#   silence            NOTSURE INITIALSILENCE       2.50
#   noise              HUMAN   SILENCEAFTERGREETING 1.42
#
# Before PR1 these mailboxes came out MACHINE - but by MAXWORDS, the very
# bug D1 removes (three word-groups), not by anything about their length.
#
# CONFIRMED ON REAL CALLS, not only in the simulator. The matrix run of
# 2026-10-24 02:30 UTC (section 3, 12 cases x 5 runs, real SIP with RTP
# audio through the lab's two FreeSWITCH instances) reproduces the
# simulator to within 20 ms:
#
#   case               real call                        amd-sim.py
#   human_hello        HUMAN  SILENCEAFTERGREETING 1.619   1.60
#   human_long         HUMAN  SILENCEAFTERGREETING 4.221   4.22
#   human_phrases      HUMAN  SILENCEAFTERGREETING 3.556   3.54   <- D1 fixed
#   silent_then_hello  NOTSURE INITIALSILENCE      2.528   2.50
#   beep_only          HUMAN  SILENCEAFTERGREETING          2.30
#   vm_beep_short      NOTSURE TOOLONG                      7.02
#   vm_beep_long       NOTSURE TOOLONG                      7.02
#   vm_beep_1200ms     NOTSURE TOOLONG                      7.02
#   vm_beep_noisy      NOTSURE TOOLONG                      7.02
#   vm_nobeep          NOTSURE TOOLONG                      7.02
#
# human_phrases is the D1 case and it is HUMAN on every run: a person who
# answers in three phrases with 0.4 s gaps is no longer a MACHINE. That
# part of PR1 works. The five mailbox rows are the cost.
#
# Why tests/amd_test.py does not show this: it drives the state machine
# from hand-written envelopes in which "voice" is continuous, so its "7 s
# greeting, no real pauses" row reaches LONGGREETING at 5720 ms. Real
# audio has word gaps; the density is about half that. This is exactly
# what the real-call lab is for.
#
# NOT changed tonight. Raising total_analysis_time is the obvious fix, but
# it is a trade Eric has to make: it is also how long a LIVE person who
# never pauses for 800 ms sits in silence before AMD gives up and sends
# them to an agent. Nothing in tonight's window depends on it - AMD is
# advisory (RUNBOOK section F), BE-17's drop is a manual button, and
# BE-12's automatic drop is not being built tonight. The safe direction is
# the one the config is already on: it will not call a person a machine.
